  "summary": "The coastal Camino de Santiago along the Bay of Biscay from Irún on the French border, through the Basque Country, Cantabria, Asturias, and into Galicia where it joins the Camino Francés at Arzúa for the final approach to Santiago de Compostela. Approximately 788 km in 34 native stages plus a 39 km shared run on the Francés to Santiago (~820 km total Irún → Santiago per Cicerone). Among the oldest Caminos — walked when inland Iberia was under Moorish control and the Atlantic coast remained the safer Christian corridor. The longest non-Francés Camino and the only major Camino that stays within ",
